\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The classical small-spacing Wilson action}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Fix a flat #{d}-dimensional torus with #{d\ge2}, a compact Lie
group #{G}, and a unitary representation #{\rho:G\to U(r)}, #{r>0}.
Choose smooth periodic Hermitian fields #{A_j(x)} such that
#{iA_j(x)} lies in the image of the derived Lie-algebra representation.
All derivative bounds are fixed
as the mesh spacing #{a\to0}; take meshes whose periods fit the
torus. Define head-directed midpoint links
##{U_j(x)=\exp\left(ia A_j(x+\tfrac a2 e_j)\right).}
With the [rightmost-first transport convention](fdip-006N), the
#{j}-then-#{k} plaquette is
##{U_{jk}(x)=U_k(x)^*U_j(x+ae_k)^*U_k(x+ae_j)U_j(x).}
Its curvature convention is
##{F_{jk}=\partial_jA_k-\partial_kA_j-i[A_j,A_k].}
The matrices #{F_{jk}} are Hermitian.}

\p{For the Wilson action
##{S_W(a)=\beta\sum_x\sum_{j<k}
 \left(1-\frac{\operatorname{Re}\operatorname{tr}U_{jk}(x)}r\right),}
one has
##{S_W(a)=\frac{\beta a^{4-d}}{2r}
 \int\sum_{j<k}\operatorname{tr}(F_{jk}(x)^2)\,dx
 +O(\beta a^{5-d}).}
This is a statement about evaluating a discrete action on a fixed
smooth field. The constant in the remainder depends on that field,
the fixed torus and the representation.}

\proof{
\p{Taylor-expand the 4 midpoint values at #{x} and use
#{e^{iaA}=I+iaA-\tfrac12a^2A^2+O(a^3)}.
The terms of order #{a} cancel. The variation of the two transverse
links contributes #{ia^2(\partial_jA_k-\partial_kA_j)};
the products of first-order terms contribute #{a^2[A_j,A_k]}.
The midpoint derivatives along each link cancel between opposite
traversals. Hence, uniformly in #{x},
##{U_{jk}(x)=I+ia^2F_{jk}(x)+O(a^3).}
To obtain the real trace to the needed order, use the exact identity
for a unitary #{r\times r} matrix:
##{1-\frac{\operatorname{Re}\operatorname{tr}U}{r}
 =\frac1{2r}\operatorname{tr}\bigl((U-I)^*(U-I)\bigr).}
Substitution yields
##{1-\frac{\operatorname{Re}\operatorname{tr}U_{jk}(x)}r
 =\frac{a^4}{2r}\operatorname{tr}(F_{jk}(x)^2)+O(a^5).}
There are #{O(a^{-d})} plaquettes. After multiplication by #{a^d},
the Riemann sum for #{\sum_{j<k}\operatorname{tr}(F_{jk}^2)}
differs from its integral by #{O(a)}.
Summing the uniform error and this Riemann-sum error proves the formula.}
}

\p{To fix a component convention, suppose the Hermitian generators
in the chosen representation satisfy
##{\operatorname{tr}(T_\alpha T_\gamma)
 =T(\rho)\delta_{\alpha\gamma},\qquad T(\rho)>0.}
Write #{F_{jk}=\sum_\alpha F_{jk}^\alpha T_\alpha} and fix
#{g>0}. Choosing
##{\beta=\frac{r a^{d-4}}{T(\rho)g^2}}
gives the classical limit
##{\begin{aligned}
S_W(a)&=\frac1{2g^2}\int
 \sum_{j<k,\alpha}(F_{jk}^\alpha)^2\,dx+O(a)\\
 &=\frac1{4g^2}\int
 \sum_{j,k,\alpha}(F_{jk}^\alpha)^2\,dx+O(a).
\end{aligned}}
The second sum counts both orders, with #{F_{kj}=-F_{jk}} and
#{F_{jj}=0}. The trace normalization is an explicit assumption;
it must not be imposed on directions annihilated by a nonfaithful
representation. For the fundamental representation of #{SU(r)}
with #{T(\rho)=1/2}, this is #{\beta=2r a^{d-4}/g^2}.
For unit-charge #{U(1)}, #{r=T(\rho)=1}, giving
#{\beta=a^{d-4}/g^2}.}

\p{The matrices #{A_j} are those appearing in the link exponential;
any coupling absorbed into them must be retained when comparing
component conventions. Wingate's equation (6.20) and Exercise 2
\citet{p. 8}{wingate2025lattice} give the Wilson-action context and
the four-dimensional fundamental normalization. The ordered expansion
and error estimate above are derived with the convention stated here.
They do not prove convergence of lattice probability measures,
renormalization or the existence of a quantum continuum theory.}
